-- title:
--   Integrality of the q-adic base change of the Deligne–Rapoport model
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N_0 \neq 0$ and a prime $q$ with $q \nmid N_0$, and let $\mathfrak{P}$ be a `DRModelPackageLevel N₀ q hqN`: a bundle of structure on the Igusa scheme `X N₀ q` and its structural morphism `DRLevel.toBase N₀ q`, which by definition is `IgusaScheme.igusaTo (N₀ * q) q` to `Spec` of the base ring `DRLevel.R q`. The fields of the package record that this morphism is proper, flat and locally of finite presentation, that `X N₀ q` is an integral scheme, that the sections of the structure sheaf on affine opens are integrally closed, a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the level-$N_0q$ modular function field together with an isomorphism onto the geometric generic fibre compatible with the Galois action on places and with $q$-expansions on the Igusa charts, smoothness of relative dimension $1$ and geometric integrality of the generic fibre over $\mathbb{Q}$, two sections $\varepsilon_\infty$, $\varepsilon_0$ of the structural morphism, and further data summarised here. Let $O$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of $q$, and let $\rho_O : \mathrm{DRLevel.R}\, q \to O$ be a ring homomorphism. Then the fibre product of `DRLevel.toBase N₀ q` with $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_O$, that is the base change of `X N₀ q` to $\operatorname{Spec} O$, is an integral scheme.
--
--   This is the statement that the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_0(N_0q)$ over the base ring of the programme stays integral after base change to any discrete valuation ring in which $q$ is a uniformiser, the level-$\Gamma_0(N_0q)$ companion of the corresponding statement at level $q$ alone. It is the starting point for the local study of the special fibre over such an $O$: it is used in the analysis of the germ of $q$ as a non-zero-divisor, of the Krull dimension of the local rings, and of the branch ideals of the two sections $\varepsilon_\infty$ and $\varepsilon_0$ along which the two components of the special fibre cross.
